se early GI Joe comics and GI  Joe cartoons  so because of him we have a character  named quick kick who is on the Joe side  he's a Japanese American he's a martial  arts master and he is so hardcore that  he never wears shoes even when the Joe's  are fighting in the snow that's awesome  on the Cobra side there was storm shadow  who out of all the villains in the  franchise has the most complex and  nuanced backstory larry Hama  incidentally is also the reason why  Marvel Comics stopped coloring their  Asian Asian American characters that  bright yellow I got the play I had the  privilege of hanging out with him in New  York a few years ago we did this panel  together and afterwards we got a meal  and he told me this story he said that  in the 80s when he was working for  Marvel back then  Marvel Comics had an actual physical  coloring department in their building  nowadays if your color is working for  Marvel you do all your work at home on  Photoshop and then just email  work in but back then they had an actual  physical covering Department and one day  Larry goes into the Marvel offices he  visits the coloring department and he  just straight up asked them why do we  color our Asian and Asian American  characters this bright yellow that is  not an actual human skin color and they  said I don't know we just always done  that and they said maybe we should stop  and they did in the 90s x-men had this  very popular TV cartoon the x-men just  like GI Joe has always been this bastion  of diversity and representation of  Asians and Asian Americans has always  been strong with this team of mutants  there's Sunfire a Japanese mutant with  the power to shoot fireballs out of his  hands there is Jubilee an Asian American  mutant who feels like somebody I grew up  with she grew up in Southern California  and she has the power to shoot fireworks  out of her hands and there's also  Psylocke now psylocke technically was  born british but in one storyline she  was kidnapped by n