  back to my own origin as the child of  immigrants when I was a kid it was hard  for me to find stories with people who  look like me  both in the books that I was reading and  in the shows that I was watching back in  the day we didn't have an awesome show  like fresh off the boat so maybe I  gravitated towards superhero comics  because they were the closest thing that  I could find superhero comics comic  books in general as a publishing format  got their start in the 1930s 1933 to be  exact this many people believe is the  very first comic book ever within about  a decade when we got into the 40s comics  had become a mass medium selling  millions and millions of copies every  month a lot of the people that we now  consider the Masters of the medium are  children of immigrants specifically they  were the sons of Jewish immigrants from  Europe there was Jack Kirby who was born  Jacob Kurtzberg the co-creator of  Fantastic Four Avengers and the x-men  Joe Shuster and Jerry Siegel created  Superman together Stan Lee he was born  Stanley Lieber co-created Fantastic Four  x-men Avengers he also co-created  spider-man will Eisner I mentioned  earlier The Godfather of American  makes Bob Kane who was born Robert Kahn  and Bill Finger created Batman together  and Harvey Kurtzman founded Mad Magazine  all comic book legends all sons of  Jewish immigrants from Europe so from  the very beginning comics was an  outsider's medium it was a way for these  poor Jewish kids to find a voice for  themselves to get their stories out into  the world now I don't know if they meant  to do this on purpose I don't know if  they're consciously doing this but they  definitely embedded pieces of their own  experience into these stories that they  were telling I'll give you an example  Superman when Jerry Siegel and Joe  Shuster first created Superman in 1938  they established a genre Superman is one  of those few characters that is popular  enough and powerful enough to create his  own category of story and when they did  this they put pieces of their own  heritage into this comic that they were  creating Superman you all know his  origin story right as an infant he's  placed into a metal basket he's launched  into space to save him from doom he  grows up to become the savior of a  people this is essentially a science  fiction version of Moses story Moses as  an infant is placed into a basket of  reeds he's launched into space I'm sorry  into a river  different Bible launched into a river to  save him from doom grows up to become  the savior of the people Superman is a  hero with superhuman strength but he has  this critical weakness that's a sum of  all the strength in his case it's  kryptonite this mirrors another story  from the Hebrew Scriptures the story of  Samson a hero with superhuman strength  he also has a critical weakness that  saps him of all his strength in his case  it's haircuts now the central reality of  Superman's life is that he lives in  between two cultures most of the time  when most people see him walking down  the streets of Metropolis they think he  is a quote-unquote average American but  deep down inside he knows he's not deep  down inside he knows he's from another  place he's from another culture that  he's a foreigner and alien an immigrant  and just to get through his daily  activities he has to hide this fact from  the people around him this idea of  hiding who you truly are that was a  daily reality for Siegel and Shuster and  all their Jewish friends and family both  here in the United States and especially  in Europe  remember when Superman was getting  started in the 1930s so was World War 2  so this idea this dynamic of being in  between cultures have having two  different identities that felt very real  to me as an Asian American I had two  different identities I had a Chinese  name that I used at home at American  name that I used at school  I spoke two different languages Mandarin  at home in English at school 