ero  books acted as my mirrors however  imperfectly they reflected parts of my  own experience back to me and they told  me that what I was going through was  important that I wasn't alone that other  people are experienced these same  emotional realities and I think that's  why as an asian-american I was drawn to  comic books I don't think I'm the only  one I think this might be another reason  why so many asian-americans are drawn to  American comics that were drawn by the  outsider leanness of the medium so  unfortunately for our parents I do think  this trend is going to continue as the  decades go on I do think more and more  Asian Americans more and more people  from outsider cultures are gonna enter  American comics and find their own  storytelling voice now let me tell you  about that character that I teased  earlier  the first asian-american superhero in my  opinion his name is the green turtle he  first debuted in 1945 in a series called  blazing comics and in a lot of ways he's  kind of a dorky character he was created  by Chu hang one of the first Chinese  Americans to be working in the American  comic book industry and he's kind of a  Batman rip off so he operates out of a  turtle cave he drives around in a turtle  plane he wears this turtle themed  costume he didn't even have a shirt  that's how dorky his costume is I read  about him and despite all the dorky  elements I was fascinated by this  character I was fascinated because there  is a rumor surrounding his creation the  rumor is that his creator Chu hing  wanted to make green turtle a  chinese-american just like he himself  was but his publishers wouldn't let him  do it his publishers were the small  company that's no longer with us they're  called rural home they didn't think that  a chinese-american superhero would be  able to sell in 1940's America so Chu  hing reacts the way all cartoonists  react when we were met with adversity he  gets really passive-aggressive and he  draws these early green turtle comics so  that you almost never see his face most  of the panels look like this all you see  is his turtle cape and then whenever he  is turned around something is obscuring  his face it's either a piece of  furniture or a character that's standing  in the way or his own arm and the rumor  is that chewing did this so that he and  his reader could imagine his character  as he originally intended as a  chinese-american I don't know if these  rumors are true but they're intriguing  enough to me that I wanted to do a book  about it so I teamed up with a friend of  mine named Sonny Liu and we created a  superhero origin for him the green  turtle only lasted five issues so true  he never gets around to confirming  whether or not he is a chinese-american  never gets around to telling us his even  his secret origin so Sonny and Liu and I  we created a secret origin for him it's  called the shadow hero and in our book  we firmly established him as a  chinese-american  I thought about why the green turtle  captured my imagination and and I think  it's this you know  asian-americans we often struggle with  this perception of Perpetual foreignness  no matter how long our families have  been here no matter whether or not we  were born here people still see us as  foreigners  I believe that superheroes are a  quintessentially American genre they  were invented here they became popular  as America was entering the world stage  I think superheroes at their best  express America at its best so when I  found out that in the earliest days of  the quintus this quintessentially  American genre there's this character  who might be a Chinese American I felt  this deep wave of affirmation I did  shadow hero out of that affirmation was  like American comic book history was  telling me there have always been people  like you here even if you had to hide  your face this is in part why I am proud  to be a part of a movement called we  need diverse books right now we are in a  sustained conversation about diversity  not just in American comics and books  but also in just American storytelling  in general throughout this presentation  I've been drawing on the work of dr.  Ruden Simms Bishop she teaches at the  Ohio State University and she wrote an  article called mirrors windows and  sliding glass doors that gave us a way  to talk about diversity in literature I  believe we need diverse books because  every reader needs books that are  mirrors and books that are windows books  that are mirrors reflect our own  experiences back to us and by doing that  they teach us to love ourselves books  that are windows give us glimpses of the  lives of people who at least on the  surface seem very different from us and  by doing that they teach us to love  others I believe that these two things  working in conjunction being able to  love other people the way we love  ourselves that is a foundation of a just  immoral society and that is why I am a  part of the reading without walls  program the way this program works is  you and your community  that a due date for yourselves and then  by the end of that due date you just  have to do one of three things one read  a book about a character who doesn't  look like you were live like you to read  a book about a topic that you don't know  much about or three read a book in a  format that you don't normally read for  fun if you really want to go for the  gold find one book that fits all three  criteria we've been running this program  for about three years now we've heard  from teachers librarians bookstore  owners who have told us that they've  been able to use this program to move  books that would otherwise not move and  that is everything that's that's what  we're going for if you want more  information please visit reading without  walls comm now let me end with this I  want to tell you that comics matter  comics matter because stories matter and  comics are a way of telling stories each  of us has this voice i