rt of his impact on Asians and  Asian Americans sort of thing so I think  you have to be careful but I do think  there's value in doing that yes  yeah good question do you have an answer  for that I love it oh okay okay  okay so so secret holders first of all  thank you for reading it see coders is a  is a middle grade series that I did with  a friend of mine named Mike Holmes or  six volumes out the sixth volume just  came out a few weeks ago and we're  trying to teach kids the fundamentals of  computer science while also telling them  an entertaining story so in that book  the main character is a young girl who's  named after probably the most important  coder in the history of computer science  a woman by the name of grace Murray  hopper and and you're also talking about  how you're kind of spoiling it that's  fine the the coders go into this  alternate dimension that is flat and in  that dimension women at the very bottom  of the social hierarchy and they're just  lines everybody else's shapes all the  men are shapes and they're just lines I  actually did not make that up so that's  actually from a science fiction classic  called called flatland  have you read flatland it's by it's by a  guy named Edwin Abbott and it's an  amazing book you all should be flattened  you could find a public domain version  of it somewhere right you can download  it for free on Kindle you can buy copies  of it like anywhere in any bookstore  will have it and it's it's a book about  it's a book about math it's a book about  religion it's a book about social  hierarchy and politics and it covers all  of it in such an amazing way but it also  it also talks about gender dynamics and  the the the commentary that I've read  said that if you look at Abbott's life  he was an advocate for girls education  when that was not a given and and people  read it as people read his his flatland  book and the fact that he made women  lines as a way of commenting on the  state of girls education during