oes have something to do  with the decline of reading you know  when you read you are really spending  quality time with a text you're really  spending quality time with that author  and and that's I think that's part of  the solution is encouraging more quality  time with people's stories yes  yeah I mean I think I think there are  two ways in which graphic novels can fit  in the American education system one is  as an on-ramp to the discipline of  reading to the habit of reading and and  we've seen that right we've there's  stories anecdotal over and over again of  kids who don't like to read who begin to  read graphic novels and are eventually  introduced to the wider world of reading  the school library journal published a  study that found that the very presence  of graphic novels in a school library  ups the circulation of non graphic  material by almost 30 percent so the  kids might come for graphic novels but  they'll stay for the other stuff as well  so that's one way the second way I do  think is that graphic novels are medium  worthy of study in and of themselves in  the modern world what I think is that  graphic novels are a great way of  training especially young readers in the  skills of visual literacy I I do think  we have to be more critical about what  we see and reading graphic novels within  a classroom setting is a great way of  training kids to do that so it sounds  like we're out of time I want to thank  you all for being here really was a  pleasure and an honor to talk to you all  thank you  you
 

